extends ../template

block variables
  - var title = 'Blog', className = 'blog'

block content
  +header('blog', 'blog', '346px', '190px')
  section.section.section_blog
    .triangles.triangles_blog
      svg.triangles__svg(xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewbox="0 0 1800 213" preserveaspectratio="none")
        polygon(points="0,0 0,213 1800,213 1800,0 900,163").triangles__svg_blog
    .blog__content-container
      #blog__sidebar.blog__col.blog__col_left
        +blog-nav-items
      .blog__col.blog__col_right
        .blog-articles
          ul.blog-articles__list
            li.blog-articles__item
              article.article
                .article__title
                  p Sass essentials
                .article__pubdate
                  span Course project notes
                .article__text
                  p Sass variables and mixins help keep recurring colours, spacing and responsive rules consistent. This portfolio uses them to share its forest palette across pages.
                  p The original stylesheet used Sass imports. The updated build uses modules with @use while retaining the original layout. The example below comes from the learning project.
                  .article__img-container
                    //.article__img
                    p.codepen(data-height='150', data-theme-id='0', data-slug-hash='ZvOOwb', data-default-tab='html', data-user='Lirra', data-embed-version='2', data-pen-title='form__reviews', data-preview='true')
                      | See the Pen
                      a(href='https://codepen.io/Lirra/pen/ZvOOwb/') form__reviews
                      |  by Iryna (
                      a(href='https://codepen.io/Lirra') @Lirra
                      | ) on
                      a(href='https://codepen.io') CodePen
                      | .
                    script(async='', src='https://production-assets.codepen.io/assets/embed/ei.js')
            li.blog-articles__item
              article.article
                .article__title
                  p Everyday layout techniques
                .article__pubdate
                  span Course project notes
                .article__text
                  p Responsive layouts need to work with different screen widths and text lengths. This project combines flexible containers, media queries and scalable images.
                  p The navigation, project slider and contact section provide examples of adapting the same content for desktop and mobile screens.
            li.blog-articles__item
              article.article
                .article__title
                  p The original Gulp workflow
                .article__pubdate
                  span Course project notes
                .article__text
                  p The original Gulp setup compiled templates and styles, bundled scripts and copied assets. It was part of the coursework workflow.
                  p The updated project uses a small Node.js build script with Pug, Sass and esbuild. Source files remain separate from the generated site.
            li.blog-articles__item
              article.article
                .article__title
                  p Templates with Jade and Pug
                .article__pubdate
                  span Course project notes
                .article__text
                  p Jade, now called Pug, provides a compact syntax for HTML templates. Shared layouts and mixins keep repeated markup in one place.
                  p This portfolio uses mixins for navigation, social links, skill charts and the footer. Updating a shared component keeps the pages consistent.
  +footer('blog')
  #blog-nav_hidden
    .blog-nav-hidden__trigger
    +blog-nav-items
